{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And one who eats of its carcass, etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said that the verse comes only to give a measure for one who touches and one who carries, namely the volume of an olive's worth. And the verse's intention is not in its simple sense — that it would render him impure in the digestive tract — for we find that it says regarding the carcass of a pure bird (Leviticus 22:8): ״לֹא יֹאכַל לְטָמְאָה בָהּ״ — through it you become impure in the digestive tract, but you do not become impure through the carcass of an animal in the digestive tract. And one must know why the Torah did not state the matter explicitly. Perhaps it is because the verse intended to include many laws that pertain to the category of eating, besides the measure of an olive's worth. They also said (Ḥullin 71a): the flesh of a creeping thing that spoiled and became foul and is disqualified from being eaten by a dog is pure — end of quote. And this matter is derived from the laws of eating, as the Tosafot wrote (Bekhorot 23a), from what the verse states (II Kings 9:10): ״בְּחֵלֶק יִזְרְעֶאל יֹאכְלוּ הַכְּלָבִים״ — the eating of dogs is thereby called eating, etc. If so, since the verse intended to equate impurity with the laws of eating — both for the measure and for other matters — therefore if it had come to teach all the details of the laws, it would have needed to add many things, and choice silver is the tongue of the righteous."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:27.839750635Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}